From 99d364db003a9b738875913c651018482efe1ad0 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: dugs <dugahol@protonmail.com>
Date: Fri, 30 Oct 2026 17:25:15 +1030
Subject: [PATCH 35/37] input: apple-z2: D21 one frame read per IRQ - drop the
 bounded drain loop

The D21 IRQ handler drained up to 8 packets per IRQ and always ended with
one extra pull that found the queue empty. That trailing pull+read is an
off-IRQ transaction pair the controller never expects: AppleMultitouchSPI
does exactly ONE readOneFrameOfData per IRQ (1 IRQ = 1 frame is the
contract; the data read is the only ACK), and the poll_hz experiment
already measured what off-schedule pulls do to this controller - all-zero
replies, stream collapse, wedge.

The drain loop survived for hours on quiet boots (steady 25 fps production
rarely leaves the queue non-empty at handler exit) and killed the stream
in 13 s - 8 min otherwise: with phantom lifecycle bursts the cap-8 exits
and trailing empty pulls race the controller's queue engine until the
assert engine stops (line high, no IRQs, scan counters still climbing, HL
state Active - captured twice today, 2026-10-30).

The drain was also unnecessary: the touch IRQ is LEVEL-LOW, so while
frames stay queued the line stays asserted and the handler re-fires - the
queue drains at the controller's own pace with zero extra transactions.
---
 drivers/input/touchscreen/apple_z2.c | 31 ++++++++++++++--------------
 1 file changed, 15 insertions(+), 16 deletions(-)

diff --git a/drivers/input/touchscreen/apple_z2.c b/drivers/input/touchscreen/apple_z2.c
index 921ad29df..ee4f74f9f 100644
--- a/drivers/input/touchscreen/apple_z2.c
+++ b/drivers/input/touchscreen/apple_z2.c
@@ -1499,25 +1499,24 @@ static irqreturn_t apple_z2_irq(int irq, void *data)
 	if (!z2->booted) {
 		return IRQ_HANDLED;
 	} else if (apple_z2_is_d21(z2)) {
-		unsigned int i;
-
 		/*
-		 * Level-low line: keep draining until the controller stops
-		 * handing out valid frames.
+		 * Kext-faithful: EXACTLY ONE frame read per IRQ
+		 * (AppleMultitouchSPI::handleInterrupt -> gated
+		 * readOneFrameOfData; "the default contract is 1 IRQ = 1
+		 * frame").  Do NOT drain in a loop here: the trailing pull
+		 * that finds the queue empty is an off-IRQ transaction the
+		 * controller never expects - the same transaction class that
+		 * wedges it when poll_hz>0 (measured 2026-10-30).  The old
+		 * bounded drain survived for hours on quiet boots and killed
+		 * the stream in minutes-to-hours otherwise (stochastic).
+		 *
+		 * The line is LEVEL-LOW: while frames stay queued the line
+		 * stays asserted and this handler re-fires, which drains the
+		 * queue at the controller's own pace with no extra
+		 * transactions.
 		 */
 		mutex_lock(&z2->io_lock);
-		for (i = 0; i < 8; i++) {
-			error = apple_z2_read_packet(z2);
-			if (error)
-				break;
-			/*
-			 * Keep draining while the controller is either handing
-			 * out frames or still servicing the queue.
-			 */
-			if (!z2->runtime_frame_valid &&
-			    !z2->runtime_control_reply)
-				break;
-		}
+		error = apple_z2_read_packet(z2);
 		mutex_unlock(&z2->io_lock);
 		if (error)
 			dev_warn_ratelimited(&z2->spidev->dev,
-- 
